A||N||6||cmyokley||Constant Idle Problem makes me sad :<(||||8
Z||000000||cmyokley||11-09-1999||04:37 PM||||I have tried to self diagnose the idle problem I have with my 86GT and am about to pull out what hair I have left. <P>While at idle the engine will idle at around 1000 RPM fairly steady for several seconds than the engine will start to increase and decrease in RPM. One cycle up and down it will change 50-100 RPM than another cycle up and down it will swing several hundred RPM than it might settle out at 1000 RPM again for several seconds than start the idle fluxuation again, this will continue till I rev the motor up. <P>I have completely rebuilt the engine, it has 600 miles on it, and during the rebuild I have replaced the<BR>-O2 sensor<BR>-both temp. sensors<BR>-IAC valve<BR>-TPS<BR>-MAP<BR>-EGR valve<BR>-EGR switch<BR>-rebuilt Distributor (from the fiero store)<BR>-added a MSD 6A with MSD "GM style" coil<BR>-plugs, wires, cap, rotor, pick up coil<BR>-fuel pump<P>I have also checked all the vacuum hoses and feel sure that they are in good shape.<P>I read one thread where the guy thought that air could be getting in at the stem for the butterfly valve at the intake? could this be a problem?<P>Has anyone had this type of problem and solved it....what else could the problem be...HELP...ME....||||reg||8
Z||000001||BENGAL4||11-09-1999||04:58 PM||||Brother Yokley,<P>My 86.5 GT did the same thing. Idle would <BR>fluctuate and it would even stall at stop lights. After I replaced the timing chain & gear and added a Superchip, it stopped. It idles right at 900 rpm. I hope this helps.<P>Bill||||reg||1
Z||000002||cmyokley||11-09-1999||05:04 PM||||Bill,<P>Thanks for the reply.<P>When I rebuilt the motor I replaced the timing chain and gears. <P>I have NOT replaced the chip....could this really be a problem...if so this could be an easy fix (for a change).<P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||11-10-1999||08:52 AM||||Do you still have the original EGR tube installed? Have you checked it for leaks?  The original is a corrugated stainless steel tube with an insulation blanket covering it that is prone to cracking from age.  This will cause the symptoms you are having.<P>Try spraying some WD-40 along the tube and under the insulation while the engine is running.  If the engine rpm races while doing this then the tube is probably cracked allowing unmetered air to bypass the throttlebody.  The O2 sensor compensates by telling the ECM to shoot more fuel to keep the fuel/air ratio in range.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||RobH||11-10-1999||01:05 PM||||EGR Tube?  Is this also on the 4cyl models?  I have an '85 Sport Coupe 4cyl 5spd that does the same thing.  I must admit, that I haven't checked all the vacuum hoses, sensors, etc...yet  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> but was wondering if I should also check the EGR tube...where ever that is.  I replaced the O2 sensor about 5k miles ago. You mention that the system will inject more fuel to keep the mixture right...I have noticed that the exhaust tends to smell a little "rich" with gas.  I notice this when I come to a stop and the exhaust "catches" up, so to speak.||||reg||5
Z||000005||theogre||11-10-1999||01:15 PM||||there is no EGR tube on the 4s. The 4 cyl EGR is internally ducted and the valve bolts to the intake manifold directly.<P>you need to do the whole vacuum list. including the base of the TBI, and the PCV valve line.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Scott2M4||11-10-1999||01:23 PM||||The previous owner of my '87 4cyl said the car had fluctuating idle problems.  After replacing the plugs, wires and ignition module, he replaced the ECM - problem fixed!||||reg||1
